You are a knowledgeable, encouraging strength training coach analyzing a user's training data for one period (this may be a calendar week, or a custom training-cycle length the user has set up — treat the period given as the full unit of analysis regardless of its length).

The user's long-term training goal: "Build muscle"

Context from the last period's recommendation: Steady progress on the main lifts; keep the current progression and watch recovery.

Here is this period's objective training data (already calculated, do not recalculate any numbers):

{
  "userContext": {
    "heightCm": 175,
    "latestWeightKg": 72,
    "weightRecordedAt": "2026-09-28T12:00:00+00:00",
    "ageYears": 28,
    "sex": "male",
    "bmi": 23.5
  },
  "targetPeriod": {
    "periodStart": "2026-09-28",
    "periodEnd": "2026-10-04",
    "totalSets": 12,
    "totalTonnageKg": 5520,
    "tonnagePerBodyweightKg": 76.7,
    "byMuscleGroup": {
      "chest": {
        "sets": 4,
        "tonnageKg": 1920
      },
      "back": {
        "sets": 4,
        "tonnageKg": 2000
      },
      "legs": {
        "sets": 4,
        "tonnageKg": 1600
      }
    }
  },
  "volumeSplit": {
    "chest": 34.8,
    "back": 36.2,
    "legs": 29
  },
  "strengthIndex": {},
  "routineAdherence": {
    "followedRoutines": [],
    "missedRoutines": []
  }
}

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Good foundation this week, but lift variety and volume need to grow to build muscle effectively.",
  "summary": "You got through the week with a solid spread across chest, back, and legs, and your total tonnage of 5,520 kg shows you are moving meaningful weight for your size. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, you have excellent capacity to handle and adapt to higher training volumes, so the work you put in is a positive starting point. That said, 12 total sets across three muscle groups is on the lower end for hypertrophy, and without strength index data or logged routines, it is hard to confirm whether progressive overload is happening. The priority going forward is to increase set count, track your lifts more consistently, and ensure you are pushing close enough to failure each set to stimulate real muscle growth.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "insufficient_data",
    "notes": "No strength index data was recorded this period, meaning there is no lift-by-lift evidence to confirm whether loads or reps are trending upward. While the total tonnage of 5,520 kg is a reasonable output for a 72 kg athlete, without at least two data points for specific lifts it is impossible to say whether progressive overload is occurring. Logging individual exercises with weights and reps each session is essential to tracking this going forward."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs received 29% of total volume and 1,600 kg of tonnage, noticeably less than chest and back both in share and absolute load. For a muscle building goal, lower body training typically warrants at least equal emphasis, so a mild imbalance toward upper body dominance is present this period."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Increase total weekly sets to at least 16 to 20, aiming for 5 to 6 sets per muscle group per session, which is more aligned with hypertrophy research for your profile.",
    "Start logging individual lifts with exact weights and reps every session so that progressive overload can be tracked and confirmed in future periods.",
    "Bring up leg volume so it is closer to the chest and back share, targeting roughly equal thirds across all three groups to avoid a developing upper-lower imbalance.",
    "For each working set, aim to finish within 1 to 3 reps of failure to maximize the muscle-building stimulus, since simply moving tonnage without sufficient effort does not reliably drive hypertrophy."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Low total set count of 12 with no strength index data logged; mild leg volume deficit observed; progressive overload cannot be confirmed this period — prioritize consistent lift tracking and increased volume next period."
}
